a former Chicago police officer risked  her life and her career to expose a  massive criminal ring in her own  department think about that for a second  undercover narcotics officer name is  Shannon Spalding says fellow cops were  planting drugs and extorting people  living in a housing project but despite  her fear of retaliation she and her  partner reported them to their  Department and the FBI former judge  police officer and host of CBS's  whistleblower series Alex Ferrara went  to Chicago to talk to Spalding for the  season two premiere it was a full-blown  war every single day soon after joining  the Chicago Police Department in 1996  Shannon Spalding drew an assignment in  one of the most violent neighborhoods in  the city it was like a movie set it was  shocking off for me it was just a  completely different world to survive  Shannon leaned on veteran cops like  Ronald Watts I thought he was battling  crime and he was doing it with finesse  and grace in 2006 a decade after Shannon  Spalding was trained by watts  she had a new assignment in the  Narcotics Division I was the undercover  I would go out I would make the  controlled narcotics purchases her  partner Danny echeverria would swoop in  and make arrests but during police  interviews something strange started  happening people would say I can't  believe you're gonna arrest me when one  of your own is actually running the  narcotics trade Shannon learned Watts  and his crew would plant drugs on  residents of the Ida B Wells projects  and extort cash even the good citizens  that live there that are law-abiding  citizens they're subjected to this we  heard people put anything from a couple  bags to enough to put you away for 10 15  20 years  in 2006 I to be Wells resident Dolores  Allen witnessed this practice firsthand  when her son's Eric was pushed up  against watts police car he goes in his  pocket brings out the drugs if you guys  I catch y'all again I'm gonna put these  drugs on your that's his exact words  Dolores says watts kept his word later  that year Zarek was arrested and charged  with felony possession of heroin and  crack cocaine he was 17 years old  Dolores says the drugs were planted by  watts I just saying all this hopes and  dreams and stuff just it just went down  to Jeff from there Shannon and her  partner would eventually learn what's  bad deeds had been going on for years if  we don't report this criminal conduct  we're absolutely no better than Watts  or any of these other corrupt officers  and if we do we may just be ending our  careers putting ourself in real danger  so what did you and your partner decide  to do I convinced him that we needed to  go to the FBI and my greatest fear was  because this was such a long-running  criminal enterprise I felt that we would  be set up for dead Shannon Spalding and  her partner spent years undercover  investigating Ronald watts and his team  with the help of that investigation more  than 60 people wrongfully arrested by  watts and his team were later exonerated  that includes a young man that you just  met in the story is Eric Johnson  Alex Ferrara joins us at the table to  discuss good to see you Alex first I'd  say thank you to Shannon Spalding number  one amazing amazing woman did she bored  did she get any backlash Oh a better  question is how much backlash she was  she lost her career she had attempts on  her life she it was just devastating  when you watch tonight's episode the  roller coaster of emotions from the the  people who were wrongfully convicted  some of them did ten years in prison for  crime they didn't commit you know and  and her coming forward against this this  code of silence I'm a former police  officer myself you know that that  doesn't happen  and police departments across the  country but it does happen in some  places and obviously Chicago was one of  them I hope it's no longer like that  well what did happen to former officer  was former officer watts was in 2012 he  was he and one of his officers were  actually arrested when they ripped off a  local drug courier he got 22 months just  22 months in prison for that but neither  he nor anybody else on his team has ever  been charged with what happened to those  so far 60 people who have been  exonerated and the number continues to  grow we'll never know how many Shannon  have any regrets well I'm sure she  regrets losing her career but she  doesn't regret coming forward and the  great thing  thank you Alex always good to have you  hysterical happy we appreciate it we do  and you have one of the best voices Alex  Ferrera I love listening to your tracks  you can watch the season 2 premiere of  whistleblower Chicago PD breaking the  code of silence at tonight at 8:00 7:00  central right here on CBS
 

